      <section class="editorial-chapter">
        <h2>The Architecture of Pay Revisions in Bihar State Administration</h2>
        <p>
          The State of Bihar manages an extensive and rapidly transforming public administration framework. With over seven lakh regular state employees—comprising the Bihar Secretariat (Sachivalaya in Patna), District Collectorates, Bihar Police, Revenue Administration, and Technical Departments—alongside more than six lakh newly inducted BPSC School Teachers and nearly four lakh pensioners, the recommendations of the <strong>8th Central Pay Commission</strong> are of profound fiscal and social importance.
        </p>
        <p>
          Historically, the Government of Bihar does not constitute an independent, multi-year pay commission that works in isolation from the national framework. Instead, once the Central Pay Commission notification is approved by the Union Cabinet and gazetted by the Ministry of Finance, the state government constitutes a high-level <strong>State Pay Revision Committee (Vetan Punarikshan Samiti)</strong> headed by the Development Commissioner or a senior retired civil servant, supported by the Secretary of the Finance Department.
        </p>
        <p>
          The mandate of the Bihar Vetan Samiti is to evaluate the state's Own Tax Revenue (OTR) generation against committed administrative expenditures, adapt central Pay Matrix levels for state cadres, and issue an official resolution (<em>Sankalp</em>). During the 7th CPC implementation, Bihar extended full parity to state civil servants with a <strong>2.57 Fitment Factor</strong> retrospectively from January 1, 2016, providing an established precedent for the upcoming 8th Pay Commission.
        </p>
      </section>

